(5600129, 809052, 94949, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by jmsetzler:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>This photo obviously meets the challenge very well :)  I don''t often grab a first place image through the Critique Club queue, but I got lucky today.  Great images are always rather easy to critique, because it''s difficult to find anything critical to say about them.  Your creative use of lighting and post processing on this image create a lot of visual and emotional impact in the story you are telling with your camera.  The theme is obvious, and I particularly like the contrast provided between life and death through the use of the old tombstone and the young girl.  On the technical side, the only suggestion I have for you would be to have used a slightly smaller aperture than the chosen f/1.4.  The focus on the young girl is strong, but in my humble opinion, I think the cross on the tombstone should get equal attention in the realm of sharpness and focus.  The camera angle with these subjects allowed the cross to fall slightly behind the depth of field with this technique.  <br/> <br/>The great images continue to flow from Iceland :)  Great work...  Kudos :) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for this John.  I agree with you.  The 1.4 narrows the DOF quite a bit and the image is certainly not without errors :-)</td>', 'Originally posted by jmsetzler: Greetings from the Critique Club... This photo obviously meets the challenge very well :)  I don''t often grab a first place image through the Critique Club queue, but I got lucky today.  Great images are always rather easy to critique, because it''s difficult to find anything critical to say about them.  Your creative use of lighting and post processing on this image create a lot of visual and emotional impact in the story you are telling with your camera.  The theme is obvious, and I particularly like the contrast provided between life and death through the use of the old tombstone and the young girl.  On the technical side, the only suggestion I have for you would be to have used a slightly smaller aperture than the chosen f/1.4.  The focus on the young girl is strong, but in my humble opinion, I think the cross on the tombstone should get equal attention in the realm of sharpness and focus.  The camera angle with these subjects allowed the cross to fall slightly behind the depth of field with this technique.   The great images continue to flow from Iceland :)  Great work...  Kudos :)   Thanks for this John.  I agree with you.  The 1.4 narrows the DOF quite a bit and the image is certainly not without errors :-)', '2009-08-05 14:14:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600142, 809212, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>As far as meeting the challenge is concerned, this one does, but it''s not extremely original.  There were several images with a similar subject, so you may have been dinged on your score due to lack of originality.<br/> <br/>The composition is OK, although the background is quite busy.  You may have been able to isolate the flowers a bit better by opening up the aperture thus blurring the background a bit better.  It would also have allowed you to use a faster shutter speed which should have given you a sharper image.<br/> <br/>Technicals.  The lighting appears to be a bit flat leaving the image a little bit dull.  You could possibly have boosted this a bit in post processing.  Speaking of post processing to my eye the image seems to be a little bit over sharp. Could just be my eyes, but...<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club As far as meeting the challenge is concerned, this one does, but it''s not extremely original.  There were several images with a similar subject, so you may have been dinged on your score due to lack of originality. The composition is OK, although the background is quite busy.  You may have been able to isolate the flowers a bit better by opening up the aperture thus blurring the background a bit better.  It would also have allowed you to use a faster shutter speed which should have given you a sharper image. Technicals.  The lighting appears to be a bit flat leaving the image a little bit dull.  You could possibly have boosted this a bit in post processing.  Speaking of post processing to my eye the image seems to be a little bit over sharp. Could just be my eyes, but... ', '2009-08-05 14:32:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600209, 809895, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First of all let me congratulate you on a good score on your first challenge submission! <br/> <br/>As far as fitting the challenge, I think it was definitely a little bit different and so likely scored well due to that.<br/> <br/>I like the composition and find the extra rock gives the image some context, but I think it may be interesting to see it without too.<br/> <br/>From a technical point of view, I find the main subject to be a bit soft. It looks to me like it has been quite heavily cropped.  Not in itself a big problem, but typically the sharper an image is, the better it will do on DPC.<br/> <br/>Overall a good first challenge entry.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  First of all let me congratulate you on a good score on your first challenge submission!  As far as fitting the challenge, I think it was definitely a little bit different and so likely scored well due to that. I like the composition and find the extra rock gives the image some context, but I think it may be interesting to see it without too. From a technical point of view, I find the main subject to be a bit soft. It looks to me like it has been quite heavily cropped.  Not in itself a big problem, but typically the sharper an image is, the better it will do on DPC. Overall a good first challenge entry.', '2009-08-05 15:37:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600232, 810157, 59029, '<td valign="top">Great work Wendy!  So why are you a TSer again? :-)</td>', 'Great work Wendy!  So why are you a TSer again? :-)', '2009-08-05 15:52:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600298, 810220, 75585, '<td valign="top">Thank you all so much!  I am shocked that this ribboned with such a low score :-)  Guess you never can tell the ways of DPC :-)  I wish it wasn''t basic editing so I could have cloned out the reflections - I really need to get a lightbox one of these days LOL  And there was a divot in the cherry that I would have loved to fix but when I flipped it around the heart was not as evident and I really wanted to highlight that. <br/> <br/>Thank you all again for your nice comments both during and after the challenge.</td>', 'Thank you all so much!  I am shocked that this ribboned with such a low score :-)  Guess you never can tell the ways of DPC :-)  I wish it wasn''t basic editing so I could have cloned out the reflections - I really need to get a lightbox one of these days LOL  And there was a divot in the cherry that I would have loved to fix but when I flipped it around the heart was not as evident and I really wanted to highlight that.  Thank you all again for your nice comments both during and after the challenge.', '2009-08-05 16:38:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600315, 809052, 75585, '<td valign="top">Gorgeous shot!  You were heads above the others in your score and well deserved.  Congratulations to you on your blue!!</td>', 'Gorgeous shot!  You were heads above the others in your score and well deserved.  Congratulations to you on your blue!!', '2009-08-05 16:53:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600317, 810157, 75585, '<td valign="top">Nice work - I love how his eyes just pop off the screen.  Congratulations on your red!!</td>', 'Nice work - I love how his eyes just pop off the screen.  Congratulations on your red!!', '2009-08-05 16:55:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600348, 810222, 87808, '<td valign="top">CC Club here,<br/> <br/>OKay I am going to go out on a limb here and guess what you where thinking when you took this shot. <br/> <br/>IT was once alive, vibrant and full of life, then it died, but now it can make life again with the seeds from the flower heads.  So in fact you have really gone for life, death and life again.  Nice job!<br/> <br/>You are tack sharp on the orange flower and its leaf, which drifts off into a smooth blur.  I think the green background is not working in this shot as it is very vibrant, maybe toning it down a little? <br/> <br/>The composition is tilted, which I actually like, I like that it inst straight on, that would make for a boring shot.<br/> <br/>I think and believe, this is exactly what you wanted to shoot and processed. <br/> <br/></td>', 'CC Club here, OKay I am going to go out on a limb here and guess what you where thinking when you took this shot.  IT was once alive, vibrant and full of life, then it died, but now it can make life again with the seeds from the flower heads.  So in fact you have really gone for life, death and life again.  Nice job! You are tack sharp on the orange flower and its leaf, which drifts off into a smooth blur.  I think the green background is not working in this shot as it is very vibrant, maybe toning it down a little?  The composition is tilted, which I actually like, I like that it inst straight on, that would make for a boring shot. I think and believe, this is exactly what you wanted to shoot and processed.  ', '2009-08-05 17:10:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600365, 809509, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hello from the CC Club, <br/> <br/>How funny, I did the same exact shot!!!<br/> <br/>the over all impression i am getting from this, is the placement could have been different.  Play around with your props you will be surprised at what comes about. Having the dead sunflower lower would have seperated the two.  DPC expects photos to be tack sharp, so in post processing try to sharpen a little at a time until you get the desired affect. <br/> <br/>There are no details in the petals which probably hurt your score a little and it seems over saturated. <br/> <br/>When you are working with a desk lamp, try different angles, don''t be afraid to have it over the top, laying on its side, hanging off your shoulder etc.  I think some of the problem may have been that you had your exposure at 2 seconds, this will let in a lot of light and it shows on your petals. <br/> <br/>Feel free to pm me if you have any questions<br/></td>', 'Hello from the CC Club,  How funny, I did the same exact shot!!! the over all impression i am getting from this, is the placement could have been different.  Play around with your props you will be surprised at what comes about. Having the dead sunflower lower would have seperated the two.  DPC expects photos to be tack sharp, so in post processing try to sharpen a little at a time until you get the desired affect.  There are no details in the petals which probably hurt your score a little and it seems over saturated.  When you are working with a desk lamp, try different angles, don''t be afraid to have it over the top, laying on its side, hanging off your shoulder etc.  I think some of the problem may have been that you had your exposure at 2 seconds, this will let in a lot of light and it shows on your petals.  Feel free to pm me if you have any questions', '2009-08-05 17:20:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(5600372, 809052, 41746, '<td valign="top">Blue ribbon every month from now on.. Völlur á þér þessa dagana, innilegar hamingjuóskir! Góð hugmynd og tæknilega vel útfærð. Congrats!</td>', 'Blue ribbon every month from now on.. Völlur á þér þessa dagana, innilegar hamingjuóskir! Góð hugmynd og tæknilega vel útfærð. Congrats!', '2009-08-05 17:27:11.000000', NULL, 0),
